## Install

1. Drop the `Scripts/` folder into your project's `Assets/`.
2. Add the `{{ s.name }}` component to the bike GameObject — needs a
   `Rigidbody2D` (added automatically via `[RequireComponent]`) and a
   `Collider2D` so it sits on the ground/ramps.
3. Tag your player `{{ s.target_tag }}`. Its own controller must expose a
   `SetMovementLocked(bool)` method (Controller.cs.j2's `2d_platformer`/
   `2d_fighting` variants already do) — this component calls it via
   `SendMessage`, so no compile-time reference is needed either direction.

## Behaviour

Walk within **{{ "%.1f"|format(s.mount_range) }}m** and press **{{ s.mount_key }}**
to mount: the rider is parented onto the bike (its own `Rigidbody2D` goes
kinematic and its controller's movement locks) and horizontal input now
drives the bike itself, up to **{{ "%.1f"|format(s.max_speed) }} m/s**.
{% if s.jump_impulse > 0 %}
Jump applies a **{{ "%.1f"|format(s.jump_impulse) }}** impulse while
grounded — enough for a hop, or to clear a gap after a ramp launch.
{% endif %}
Press **{{ s.mount_key }}** again to dismount: the rider unparents, its
controller unlocks, and the bike sits still until mounted again.

A ramp (an ordinary prop entity whose name/model_hint mentions "ramp" — see
`unity_pipeline.py`'s `_apply_ramp_tilts`) needs no special handling here:
ordinary 2D physics launches a fast-moving bike off an angled collider on
its own.
